* dfs-anagrams: don't show dense cache details if dense cache isn't chosen
* -S 0 (auto) default (hardware threads)
* --require-completable auto
* -P 10000 default
* filter index entries containing configurable "skip words" during phase 1,
  especially `the`
* add -d/--dict option to top-segments, first-segments, filter-segments
* dfs-anagrams --wf -t FULLY-SCOPED-TARGET should infer -u from the target;
  currently it must still be supplied explicitly
* decide the BEST-pair bonus for dfs-anagrams invocations without -g; the
  dynamic scheme in plans/dynamic-best-bonus.md deliberately uses N from
  -g N and leaves the unbounded-search behavior unchanged for now
* move argument parsing out of filter-segments, first-segments, and any other
  tools with inline parsing into separate parse_args functions
* use the optparse header/library for option parsing in those tools
* consider writing rerank_all output, and possibly top_all output, to a
  temporary file and renaming it only after successful completion (like wf's
  place())
* investigate p1 eval's "previously filtered pairs" check: it probably uses
  comm on ordered pair text, so reversed pair orderings are not recognized and
  some pairs are evaluated twice
* convert all enums to enum class where appropriate
* after the shared pair-filter struct lands, consolidate top-segments' filter
  attribution and filter-segments' boolean rejection behind one implementation
  of the same precedence: classified_no, target_no, explicit reject, allowlist,
  then dictionary.  For example:
    enum PairFilterLayer { PAIR_FILTER_NONE, PAIR_FILTER_CLASSIFIED_NO, ... };
    PairFilterLayer first_rejecting_layer(
        std::string const& segment) const;
  top-segments can use the returned layer for its per-layer counters, while
  filter-segments can collapse it to a boolean.  Keep this change separate from
  packaging the filter data in the shared struct.
